\n\n“जो भक्त एकान्त भाव से भगवान् नारायण की भक्ति में लगे रहते हैं, वे जीवन की किसी\nअवस्था में कभी भयभीत नहीं होते। उनके लिए स्वर्ग, मोक्ष तथा नरक एक से हैं, क्योंकि ऐसे भक्त\nभगवान् की सेवा में ही लगे रहना चाहते हैं।”",
      "textEn": "390\n\nThey do not turn away in silence, being devoted to others. Without night, without miserliness, the one who seeks liberation,\nThere is no other refuge for him, I see the delusion. 44). Meaning of terms\nFor the purposes of the Generally; Dev—God; Munayah—great saints; Sw—Private; Vimukti-Kamah—those who desire liberation from this\nphysical world; Maunam—with a silent attitude; Charanti—they wander (in the Himalayan forests where\nthere is no contact with the activities of materialists); Vijane—in a secluded place; Na—not; Par-Artha-Nishthaah—one who is interested in working for others to benefit from the Krishna consciousness movement; Na—not; Etan—these;\nVihaya—leaving; Krupanan—interested in working for others to benefit from the Krishna consciousness movement; Na—not; Etan—them;\nVihaya—leaving; Krupanan—fools and cunning people (who, without knowing the benefit of human life, remain engaged in material work Are);\nVimu mukse—I want to be free and return to the abode of the Lord; Ekah—alone; Na—not; Anyam—another; Tvat—for you;\nHi—for this; Asya—belonging to this; Sharanam—refuge; Bhramatha—of the being wandering and roaming in the universe; Anupashye—I will see.\nOh Lord Nrusimhadeva, I see that there are many saintly men, but they are interested in their own salvation. Without caring for big cities, they go to the Himalayas or the forest to meditate by observing a vow of silence; they are not interested in the liberation of others, but as far as\nmy point is, I do not want my liberation alone except for these poor fools and cunning men. I\nknow that without Krishna consciousness and without taking refuge in your lotus feet,\nno one can be happy. Therefore, I want to bring all of them back to the refuge of your lotus feet.\nI want to bring them back. Meaning: This is the decision of a Vaishnava, that is, a pure devotee of God. If he has to stop in this physical world,\nthen he has no problems because his only business is\nto remain in Krishna consciousness. A person who is absorbed in Krishna consciousness can remain happy even after going to hell.\nTherefore, Prahlada Maharaj said—Naivodvije par duratyayavetaranyah—Oh, great man! I am not at all afraid of this physical world. A pure devotee is not sad in any state of life. Its confirmation\nhas been in Shrimadbhagavatam (6.17.28)—\n\nNarayanaparah: Sarve na kutashchana bibhyanti. Even those who see the meaning of the heavenly abode are equal. “Those devotees who remain engaged in the devotion of Lord Narayan with a sense of solitude, they are never afraid in any\nstage of life. For them, heaven, salvation, and hell are one and the same, because such devotees\nwant to remain engaged only in the service of God.”"
